10:22 AM
Claude responded: The issue is that backdrop-filter and backdrop-blur-lg have nothing to blur here — they only work when there's content behind the element, and they require the…
The issue is that backdrop-filter and backdrop-blur-lg have nothing to blur here — they only work when there's content behind the element, and they require the element to have a background with some transparency to show the blur effect.

But the more likely reason it "isn't working" visually is a combination of issues:

1. Missing semi-transparent background backdrop-blur blurs whatever is behind the element, but without a translucent background, you can't see the blur effect.

html
<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 text-lg backdrop-blur-lg bg-white/30">
2. backdrop-filter is not a Tailwind class In Tailwind, you don't need backdrop-filter as a separate class — backdrop-blur-lg enables it automatically in Tailwind v3+. In older versions (v2), you needed it, but it's redundant now and can be safely removed.

3. No positioning or size The div has no width/height or positioning context, so it may not be visible or covering anything.

Fixed version:

html
<div class="flex flex-col gap-6 text-lg backdrop-blur-lg bg-white/20 p-6 rounded-xl">
  <a href="#">Home</a>
  <a href="#">Why Us</a>
  <a href="#">What We Offer</a>
  <a href="#">Contact</a>
</div>
Key fixes:

bg-white/20 — adds a semi-transparent background so blur is visible
Removed redundant backdrop-filter
Added p-6 rounded-xl for basic structure
Also make sure there's actually content/an image behind this element — otherwise there's nothing to blur.

